Chapter: FireworksShow

 The skyline is BUILT here, not in the browser. The page's claim is that the
 pictures are compiled rather than hand-written, and a city drawn by a
 JavaScript function is a hole straight through it: the compute kernel would be
 Codex and everything the viewer actually recognises as a city would not be.
 So this chapter is the geometry, compiled to WebAssembly by codex/plugs/wasm,
 and the page is left holding a vertex buffer it did not compose.

 It emits a TRIANGLE mesh rather than a rectangle list. Landmarks are the
 reason: a pyramid, a dome, a splayed leg and a tapered crown are not
 rectangles, and stacking narrowing boxes to imitate one is what makes a
 skyline read as a bar chart.

 Everything is integer. Coordinates are in 1/16 of a pixel so a taper or a
 dome has somewhere to put its fractions, colours are 0..1000 per channel, and
 the trig below returns 1/1000, which is the same fixed point the GPU kernel
 beside this file uses.

 Memory, as byte offsets into WASM linear memory. The page reads all of it:

   131072  vertex count written by build-city
   131076  RNG state
   262144  vertex data, 8 words per vertex, 12000 vertices maximum
             x, y, r, g, b, kind, phase, spare
             kind 0 masonry, 1 window, 2 aviation beacon

 We say:

Section: Memory

  w32 : Integer, Integer -> Integer
  w32 (addr) (val) = poke-32 0 addr val

  rd : Integer -> Integer
  rd (addr) = peek-32 0 addr

  vtx-count-addr : Integer = 131072
  roof-count-addr : Integer = 131080
  vtx-base : Integer = 262144
  vtx-stride : Integer = 32
  vtx-max : Integer = 60000

 Rooftops, so the page launches shells off the buildings it is drawing rather
 than off a straight line it picked. Two words per roof, x then y.

  roof-base : Integer = 196608
  roof-max : Integer = 128

  roof-add : Integer, Integer -> Integer
  roof-add (x) (y) =
    let c = rd roof-count-addr
    in if c >= roof-max then c
    else let a = w32 (roof-base + c * 8) x
    in let b = w32 (roof-base + c * 8 + 4) y
    in w32 roof-count-addr (c + 1)

Section: Screen

 Sim space is 1024 by 768 pixels, held here in 1/16 of a pixel.

  q : Integer = 16
  sw : Integer = 16384
  sh : Integer = 12288
  horizon : Integer = 9024

Section: Random

 A hash rather than a stream, so a city is a pure function of its seed and the
 same seed rebuilds the same skyline on any machine.

  hsh : Integer -> Integer
  hsh (n) =
    let a = bit-xor (n * 374761393 + 668265263) (bit-shru n 13)
    in let b = bit-xor (a * 1274126177) (bit-shru a 16)
    in bit-and b 1048575

  hsh-range : Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  hsh-range (n) (lo) (hi) =
    if hi <= lo then lo else lo + (hsh n) - ((hsh n) / (hi - lo)) * (hi - lo)

Section: Fixed-Point Trig

 Milliradian in, 1/1000 out.

  tw : Integer -> Integer
  tw (a) = let m = a - (a / 6283) * 6283 in if m < 0 then m + 6283 else m

  sin-core : Integer -> Integer
  sin-core (x) =
    let x2 = x * x / 1000
    in let x3 = x2 * x / 1000
    in let x5 = x3 * x2 / 1000
    in x - x3 / 6 + x5 / 120

  tsin : Integer -> Integer
  tsin (raw) =
    let a = tw raw
    in if a <= 1570 then sin-core a
    else if a <= 3141 then sin-core (3141 - a)
    else if a <= 4712 then 0 - sin-core (a - 3141)
    else 0 - sin-core (6283 - a)

  tcos : Integer -> Integer
  tcos (raw) = tsin (raw + 1570)

Section: Vertex Emission

 Each of these takes the running vertex index and returns the next one, so a
 shape is a chain of calls and the count falls out of the last.

  emit-v : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  emit-v (n) (x) (y) (r) (g) (b) (kind) (phase) =
    if n >= vtx-max then n
    else let o = vtx-base + n * vtx-stride
    in let a = w32 o x
    in let bb = w32 (o + 4) y
    in let c = w32 (o + 8) r
    in let d = w32 (o + 12) g
    in let e = w32 (o + 16) b
    in let f = w32 (o + 20) kind
    in let g2 = w32 (o + 24) phase
    in let h = w32 (o + 28) 0
    in n + 1

  emit-tri : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  emit-tri (n) (x0) (y0) (x1) (y1) (x2) (y2) (r) (g) (b) (kind) (phase) =
    emit-v (emit-v (emit-v n x0 y0 r g b kind phase) x1 y1 r g b kind phase) x2 y2 r g b kind phase

 Four corners, top-left first and clockwise, with the top and bottom colours
 given separately so a facade can be graded up its own height.

  emit-quad : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  emit-quad (n) (x0) (y0) (x1) (y1) (x2) (y2) (x3) (y3) (rt) (gt) (bt) (rb) (gb) (bb) (kind) (phase) =
    let a = emit-v n x0 y0 rt gt bt kind phase
    in let b = emit-v a x1 y1 rt gt bt kind phase
    in let c = emit-v b x2 y2 rb gb bb kind phase
    in let d = emit-v c x0 y0 rt gt bt kind phase
    in let e = emit-v d x2 y2 rb gb bb kind phase
    in emit-v e x3 y3 rb gb bb kind phase

  emit-rect : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  emit-rect (n) (x) (y) (w) (h) (r) (g) (b) (kind) (phase) =
    emit-quad n x y (x + w) y (x + w) (y + h) x (y + h) r g b r g b kind phase

  emit-rect-g : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  emit-rect-g (n) (x) (y) (w) (h) (rt) (gt) (bt) (rb) (gb) (bb) =
    emit-quad n x y (x + w) y (x + w) (y + h) x (y + h) rt gt bt rb gb bb 0 0

 A centred trapezoid. Every tower, spire, obelisk and crown in the file is one
 or a stack of them.

  emit-taper : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  emit-taper (n) (cx) (yt) (wt) (yb) (wb) (rt) (gt) (bt) (rb) (gb) (bb) =
    emit-quad n (cx - wt / 2) yt (cx + wt / 2) yt (cx + wb / 2) yb (cx - wb / 2) yb rt gt bt rb gb bb 0 0

 A half-ellipse fan, for the domes and the saucer.

  emit-dome : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  emit-dome (n) (cx) (cy) (rx) (ry) (r) (g) (b) (i) (seg) =
    if i >= seg then n
    else let a0 = 3141 + i * 3141 / seg
    in let a1 = 3141 + (i + 1) * 3141 / seg
    in let x0 = cx + (tcos a0) * rx / 1000
    in let y0 = cy + (tsin a0) * ry / 1000
    in let x1 = cx + (tcos a1) * rx / 1000
    in let y1 = cy + (tsin a1) * ry / 1000
    in let m = emit-tri n cx cy x0 y0 x1 y1 r g b 0 0
    in emit-dome m cx cy rx ry r g b (i + 1) seg

Section: Palette

 Masonry is a cold near-black that the burst light in the page's fragment
 shader lifts. The three shades are the base, the roofline, and the stone the
 monuments are cut from, which is warmer and far lighter because those two are
 floodlit in life and read wrong at facade darkness.

  mas-r : Integer = 22
  mas-g : Integer = 24
  mas-b : Integer = 40

  top-r : Integer = 58
  top-g : Integer = 62
  top-b : Integer = 96

  stone-r : Integer = 300
  stone-g : Integer = 290
  stone-b : Integer = 260

  stone-hi-r : Integer = 400
  stone-hi-g : Integer = 390
  stone-hi-b : Integer = 350

  beacon-r : Integer = 950
  beacon-g : Integer = 160
  beacon-b : Integer = 100

  emit-beacon : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  emit-beacon (n) (x) (y) (s) (phase) =
    emit-rect n (x - s) (y - s) (s * 2) (s * 2) beacon-r beacon-g beacon-b 2 phase

Section: Depth Ranks

 Three rows of city, drawn back to front. Without them a skyline is one line of
 boxes on a flat backdrop, which is the cardboard-cutout look: everything the
 same darkness at the same distance with nothing behind it.

 Rank decides four things at once, and it is the combination that reads as
 depth rather than any one of them. Distant buildings are LIGHTER, not darker,
 because the air between is full of city glow, which is the whole of
 atmospheric perspective and the part that gets left out. They are also
 shorter on screen, narrower, and their windows are dimmer and denser.

  rank-base-r : Integer -> Integer
  rank-base-r (k) = if k == 0 then 46 else if k == 1 then 30 else 15

  rank-base-g : Integer -> Integer
  rank-base-g (k) = if k == 0 then 52 else if k == 1 then 34 else 17

  rank-base-b : Integer -> Integer
  rank-base-b (k) = if k == 0 then 86 else if k == 1 then 58 else 31

  rank-top-r : Integer -> Integer
  rank-top-r (k) = if k == 0 then 78 else if k == 1 then 54 else 34

  rank-top-g : Integer -> Integer
  rank-top-g (k) = if k == 0 then 86 else if k == 1 then 60 else 38

  rank-top-b : Integer -> Integer
  rank-top-b (k) = if k == 0 then 130 else if k == 1 then 96 else 62

 How bright a lit window is at that distance, in percent.

  rank-win : Integer -> Integer
  rank-win (k) = if k == 0 then 42 else if k == 1 then 72 else 100

Section: Windows

 A real facade is a GRID, and the grid is most of what makes a building look
 like a building. The pitch is 7 by 9 pixels rather than 13 by 15, which is
 roughly four times as many openings, and a lit one is 3 by 4 with the rest of
 the cell left as the gap between them. Two tempers, a warm office and a cold
 one, and a run of consecutive cells shares a temper so a floor reads as a
 floor rather than as confetti.

 A window is its own emitter in the page, so these colours are light rather
 than surface.

  win-pitch-x : Integer = 112
  win-pitch-y : Integer = 144

  win-cols : Integer -> Integer
  win-cols (w) = let c = (w - 6 * q) / win-pitch-x in if c < 1 then 1 else c

  win-rows : Integer -> Integer
  win-rows (h) = let r = (h - 12 * q) / win-pitch-y in if r < 1 then 1 else r

  emit-windows : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  emit-windows (n) (x) (top) (seed) (k) (cols) (rows) (rank) (guard) (spare) =
    if k >= cols * rows then n
    else if guard <= 0 then n
    else if n >= vtx-max then n
    else let cc = k - (k / cols) * cols
    in let rr = k / cols
    in let lit = hsh (seed + k * 2654435 + x)
 A whole floor is dark now and then, which is what an office block at night
 looks like and what a per-cell hash on its own never produces.
    in let floor-dark = bit-and (hsh (seed + rr * 7919)) 7
    in if floor-dark == 0 then emit-windows n x top seed (k + 1) cols rows rank (guard - 1) spare
    else if bit-and lit 15 >= 7 then emit-windows n x top seed (k + 1) cols rows rank (guard - 1) spare
    else let wx = x + 3 * q + cc * win-pitch-x
    in let wy = top + 7 * q + rr * win-pitch-y
    in let warm = bit-and (hsh (seed + rr * 31 + cc / 3)) 3
    in let br = (430 + bit-and (bit-shru lit 11) 340) * rank-win rank / 100
    in let wr = if warm == 0 then br * 60 / 100 else br
    in let wg = br * 78 / 100
    in let wb = if warm == 0 then br else br * 40 / 100
    in let m = emit-rect n wx wy (3 * q) (4 * q) wr wg wb 1 0
    in emit-windows m x top seed (k + 1) cols rows rank (guard - 1) spare

Section: Buildings

 A tower is a graded box, and above a certain height it sets back twice and
 carries a mast, which is most of what separates a skyline from a bar chart.

 A flat rectangle of one colour is a cutout. Three things fix that and they
 are all cheap: the facade is graded up its height, a narrow strip down the
 left edge is lighter so the building has a lit corner and therefore a
 direction, and a darker strip down the right gives it a shaded one. That is
 a box with a light source rather than a silhouette.

  emit-tower : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  emit-tower (n) (x) (w) (h) (seed) (rank) =
    let top = horizon - h
    in let shade = bit-and (hsh (seed + 71)) 15
    in let br = rank-base-r rank + shade
    in let bg = rank-base-g rank + shade
    in let bb = rank-base-b rank + shade * 2
    in let tr = rank-top-r rank + shade
    in let tg = rank-top-g rank + shade
    in let tb = rank-top-b rank + shade * 2
    in let body = emit-rect-g n x top w (h + 4 * q) tr tg tb br bg bb
    in let edge = 2 * q
    in let litc = emit-rect-g body x top edge (h + 4 * q) (tr * 190 / 100) (tg * 190 / 100) (tb * 175 / 100) (br * 210 / 100) (bg * 210 / 100) (bb * 190 / 100)
    in let dark = emit-rect-g litc (x + w - edge) top edge (h + 4 * q) (tr * 55 / 100) (tg * 55 / 100) (tb * 60 / 100) (br * 50 / 100) (bg * 50 / 100) (bb * 55 / 100)
    in let ledged = emit-ledges dark x top w h seed rank 0
    in let cols = win-cols w
    in let rows = win-rows h
    in let lit = emit-windows ledged x top seed 0 cols rows rank 900 0
    in let rf = roof-add (x + w / 2) top
    in emit-roof lit x top w h seed rank

 A mechanical band every eight floors or so, lighter than the facade. It is
 the horizontal line that stops a tall building being a smooth column.

  emit-ledges : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  emit-ledges (n) (x) (top) (w) (h) (seed) (rank) (i) =
    if i >= 6 then n
    else let y = top + (i + 1) * h / 7
    in if y >= top + h then n
    else if n >= vtx-max then n
    else let c = bit-and (hsh (seed + i * 337)) 3
    in if c == 0 then emit-ledges n x top w h seed rank (i + 1)
    else let lr = rank-top-r rank * 150 / 100
    in let lg = rank-top-g rank * 150 / 100
    in let lb = rank-top-b rank * 150 / 100
    in emit-ledges (emit-rect n x y w (2 * q) lr lg lb 0 0) x top w h seed rank (i + 1)

 Rooftops carry things. A bare parapet on every building is the other half of
 the cutout look, and the clutter is what the eye reads as "somebody works
 there": a water tank on legs, a mechanical penthouse, a mast, a stub aerial.

  emit-roof : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  emit-roof (n) (x) (top) (w) (h) (seed) (rank) =
    let cx = x + w / 2
    in let tr = rank-top-r rank
    in let tg = rank-top-g rank
    in let tb = rank-top-b rank
    in let pick = bit-and (hsh (seed + 913)) 7
 A parapet lip on every roof, one pixel proud of the facade and lighter.
    in let lip = emit-rect n (x - q) (top - 2 * q) (w + 2 * q) (3 * q) (tr * 160 / 100) (tg * 160 / 100) (tb * 160 / 100) 0 0
    in if h > 300 * q then
      let a = emit-taper lip cx (top - 26 * q) (w * 62 / 100) top w tr tg tb tr tg tb
      in let b = emit-taper a cx (top - 46 * q) (w * 34 / 100) (top - 26 * q) (w * 62 / 100) tr tg tb tr tg tb
      in let c = emit-rect b (cx - 2 * q) (top - 92 * q) (4 * q) (46 * q) tr tg tb 0 0
      in emit-beacon c cx (top - 94 * q) (2 * q) (bit-and (hsh (seed + 7)) 999)
    else if pick == 0 then emit-water-tank lip cx top tr tg tb
    else if pick == 1 then
      let a = emit-rect lip (cx - w / 6) (top - 13 * q) (w / 3) (13 * q) tr tg tb 0 0
      in emit-rect a (cx + w / 8) (top - 20 * q) (2 * q) (20 * q) tr tg tb 0 0
    else if pick == 2 then
      let a = emit-rect lip (cx - 5 * q) (top - 30 * q) (10 * q) (14 * q) tr tg tb 0 0
      in let b = emit-rect a (cx - q) (top - 44 * q) (2 * q) (44 * q) tr tg tb 0 0
      in emit-beacon b cx (top - 46 * q) (q + q / 2) (bit-and (hsh (seed + 23)) 999)
    else if pick == 3 then emit-water-tank (emit-rect lip (x + 3 * q) (top - 9 * q) (w / 4) (9 * q) tr tg tb 0 0) (x + w * 3 / 4) top tr tg tb
    else if pick == 4 then
      emit-rect lip (x + w / 5) (top - 7 * q) (w * 3 / 5) (7 * q) tr tg tb 0 0
    else lip

 A rooftop water tank: a barrel on four legs. The shape is unmistakable at
 this size and it is the single most city-looking object on a roof.

  emit-water-tank : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  emit-water-tank (n) (cx) (top) (tr) (tg) (tb) =
    let legs = emit-rect (emit-rect n (cx - 6 * q) (top - 9 * q) (2 * q) (9 * q) (tr * 70 / 100) (tg * 70 / 100) (tb * 70 / 100) 0 0) (cx + 4 * q) (top - 9 * q) (2 * q) (9 * q) (tr * 70 / 100) (tg * 70 / 100) (tb * 70 / 100) 0 0
    in let drum = emit-rect legs (cx - 8 * q) (top - 24 * q) (16 * q) (15 * q) (tr * 125 / 100) (tg * 122 / 100) (tb * 115 / 100) 0 0
    in emit-tri drum (cx - 8 * q) (top - 24 * q) (cx + 8 * q) (top - 24 * q) cx (top - 32 * q) (tr * 150 / 100) (tg * 146 / 100) (tb * 135 / 100) 0 0

 Height is distributed on a CUBE, not a square. Squaring put the median tower
 at 152 pixels against a 400 pixel ceiling, so nearly every building landed in
 the same band and the skyline read as a wall of equal blocks. Cubing drops the
 median to about 110 and makes the top of the range rare, which is the shape a
 skyline actually has: a low mass with a few things standing well out of it.

 Rank 0 is the far bank: narrow, short, packed shoulder to shoulder with a
 negative gap so they overlap and read as a mass rather than as a comb. Rank 2
 is the near one and carries the height.

  rank-min-w : Integer -> Integer
  rank-min-w (k) = if k == 0 then 14 * q else if k == 1 then 20 * q else 28 * q

  rank-span-w : Integer -> Integer
  rank-span-w (k) = if k == 0 then 22 * q else if k == 1 then 40 * q else 66 * q

  rank-min-h : Integer -> Integer
  rank-min-h (k) = if k == 0 then 40 * q else if k == 1 then 52 * q else 62 * q

  rank-span-h : Integer -> Integer
  rank-span-h (k) = if k == 0 then 180 * q else if k == 1 then 300 * q else 440 * q

  rank-gap : Integer -> Integer
  rank-gap (k) = if k == 0 then 0 - q else if k == 1 then 2 * q else 5 * q

  emit-row : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  emit-row (n) (x) (seed) (guard) (rank) =
    if x >= sw + 30 * q then n
    else if guard <= 0 then n
    else if n >= vtx-max then n
    else let w = rank-min-w rank + hsh-range (x + seed) 0 (rank-span-w rank)
    in let hr = hsh-range (x * 3 + seed + 11) 0 1000
    in let h = rank-min-h rank + hr * hr / 1000 * hr / 1000 * (rank-span-h rank) / 1000
    in let m = emit-tower n x w h (seed + x) rank
    in let nx = x + w + rank-gap rank + hsh-range (x + seed + 3) 0 (7 * q)
    in emit-row m nx seed (guard - 1) rank

Section: Landmarks

 Read as silhouettes at roughly 500 pixels of height. The test is whether the
 outline alone names the city.

  emit-empire : Integer, Integer -> Integer
  emit-empire (n) (cx) =
    let a = emit-rect-g n (cx - 66 * q) (horizon - 250 * q) (132 * q) (254 * q) top-r top-g top-b mas-r mas-g mas-b
    in let b = emit-rect-g a (cx - 48 * q) (horizon - 340 * q) (96 * q) (92 * q) top-r top-g top-b mas-r mas-g mas-b
    in let c = emit-rect-g b (cx - 33 * q) (horizon - 408 * q) (66 * q) (70 * q) top-r top-g top-b mas-r mas-g mas-b
    in let d = emit-taper c cx (horizon - 452 * q) (30 * q) (horizon - 408 * q) (66 * q) 120 126 175 top-r top-g top-b
    in let e = emit-rect-g d (cx - 15 * q) (horizon - 486 * q) (30 * q) (36 * q) 120 126 175 top-r top-g top-b
    in let f = emit-taper e cx (horizon - 512 * q) (9 * q) (horizon - 486 * q) (30 * q) 120 126 175 120 126 175
    in let g = emit-rect f (cx - 2 * q) (horizon - 566 * q) (5 * q) (56 * q) top-r top-g top-b 0 0
    in emit-beacon g cx (horizon - 570 * q) (2 * q) 0

 Nine bundled tubes topping out at four heights, which is the whole of what
 makes the Willis silhouette recognisable.

  willis-top : Integer -> Integer
  willis-top (i) =
    if i == 0 then 212 else if i == 1 then 300 else if i == 2 then 386
    else if i == 3 then 300 else 212

  emit-willis-tubes : Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  emit-willis-tubes (n) (cx) (i) =
    if i >= 5 then n
    else let w = 26 * q
    in let x = cx - 65 * q + i * 27 * q
    in let t = willis-top i
    in emit-willis-tubes (emit-rect-g n x (horizon - t * q) w (t * q + 4 * q)
        (if i == 2 then 78 else top-r) top-g top-b mas-r mas-g mas-b) cx (i + 1)

  emit-willis : Integer, Integer -> Integer
  emit-willis (n) (cx) =
    let a = emit-willis-tubes n cx 0
    in let b = emit-rect a (cx - 17 * q) (horizon - 458 * q) (4 * q) (74 * q) top-r top-g top-b 0 0
    in let c = emit-rect b (cx + 13 * q) (horizon - 458 * q) (4 * q) (74 * q) top-r top-g top-b 0 0
    in let d = emit-beacon c (cx - 15 * q) (horizon - 462 * q) (2 * q) 350
    in emit-beacon d (cx + 15 * q) (horizon - 462 * q) (2 * q) 800

  emit-pyramid : Integer, Integer -> Integer
  emit-pyramid (n) (cx) =
    let a = emit-rect-g n (cx - 13 * q) (horizon - 96 * q) (26 * q) (100 * q) top-r top-g top-b mas-r mas-g mas-b
    in let b = emit-tri a cx (horizon - 420 * q) (cx - 46 * q) (horizon - 96 * q) (cx + 46 * q) (horizon - 96 * q) 46 50 82 0 0
    in let c = emit-tri b cx (horizon - 420 * q) (cx + 46 * q) (horizon - 96 * q) (cx + 20 * q) (horizon - 96 * q) 74 78 118 0 0
    in let d = emit-rect-g c (cx - 50 * q) (horizon - 210 * q) (9 * q) (116 * q) mas-r mas-g mas-b mas-r mas-g mas-b
    in let e = emit-rect-g d (cx + 41 * q) (horizon - 210 * q) (9 * q) (116 * q) mas-r mas-g mas-b mas-r mas-g mas-b
    in let f = emit-taper e cx (horizon - 462 * q) (2 * q) (horizon - 420 * q) (9 * q) 120 126 175 top-r top-g top-b
    in emit-beacon f cx (horizon - 464 * q) (2 * q) 150

  emit-needle : Integer, Integer -> Integer
  emit-needle (n) (cx) =
    let a = emit-quad n (cx - 9 * q) (horizon - 300 * q) (cx + 9 * q) (horizon - 300 * q) (cx + 46 * q) horizon (cx + 28 * q) horizon top-r top-g top-b mas-r mas-g mas-b 0 0
    in let b = emit-quad a (cx - 9 * q) (horizon - 300 * q) (cx + 9 * q) (horizon - 300 * q) (cx - 28 * q) horizon (cx - 46 * q) horizon top-r top-g top-b mas-r mas-g mas-b 0 0
    in let c = emit-rect-g b (cx - 7 * q) (horizon - 300 * q) (14 * q) (300 * q) top-r top-g top-b mas-r mas-g mas-b
    in let d = emit-dome c cx (horizon - 306 * q) (58 * q) (15 * q) top-r top-g top-b 0 18
    in let e = emit-rect-g d (cx - 58 * q) (horizon - 322 * q) (116 * q) (17 * q) 120 126 175 top-r top-g top-b
    in let f = emit-dome e cx (horizon - 322 * q) (40 * q) (16 * q) 120 126 175 0 18
    in let g = emit-taper f cx (horizon - 400 * q) (2 * q) (horizon - 338 * q) (7 * q) top-r top-g top-b top-r top-g top-b
    in emit-beacon g cx (horizon - 402 * q) (2 * q) 500

  emit-monument : Integer, Integer -> Integer
  emit-monument (n) (cx) =
    let a = emit-taper n cx (horizon - 400 * q) (19 * q) horizon (30 * q) stone-hi-r stone-hi-g stone-hi-b stone-r stone-g stone-b
    in let b = emit-tri a cx (horizon - 432 * q) (cx - 9 * q) (horizon - 400 * q) (cx + 9 * q) (horizon - 400 * q) stone-hi-r stone-hi-g stone-hi-b 0 0
    in let c = emit-beacon b (cx - 5 * q) (horizon - 396 * q) (2 * q) 0
    in emit-beacon c (cx + 5 * q) (horizon - 396 * q) (2 * q) 500

  emit-columns : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  emit-columns (n) (cx) (i) (seg) =
    if i >= seg then n
    else emit-columns (emit-rect n (cx - 37 * q + i * 8 * q - i * q / 2) (horizon - 114 * q) (3 * q) (34 * q) stone-hi-r stone-hi-g stone-hi-b 0 0) cx (i + 1) seg

  emit-capitol : Integer, Integer -> Integer
  emit-capitol (n) (cx) =
    let a = emit-rect-g n (cx - 130 * q) (horizon - 52 * q) (260 * q) (56 * q) stone-r stone-g stone-b 200 194 174
    in let b = emit-rect-g a (cx - 62 * q) (horizon - 78 * q) (124 * q) (30 * q) stone-hi-r stone-hi-g stone-hi-b stone-r stone-g stone-b
    in let c = emit-rect-g b (cx - 40 * q) (horizon - 118 * q) (80 * q) (42 * q) stone-r stone-g stone-b stone-r stone-g stone-b
    in let d = emit-columns c cx 0 9
    in let e = emit-dome d cx (horizon - 118 * q) (40 * q) (46 * q) stone-hi-r stone-hi-g stone-hi-b 0 22
    in let f = emit-rect-g e (cx - 8 * q) (horizon - 178 * q) (16 * q) (16 * q) stone-hi-r stone-hi-g stone-hi-b stone-r stone-g stone-b
    in emit-taper f cx (horizon - 192 * q) (3 * q) (horizon - 178 * q) (9 * q) stone-hi-r stone-hi-g stone-hi-b stone-hi-r stone-hi-g stone-hi-b

  emit-landmark : Integer, Integer -> Integer
  emit-landmark (n) (city) =
    if city == 0 then emit-empire n (512 * q)
    else if city == 1 then emit-willis n (512 * q)
    else if city == 2 then emit-pyramid n (500 * q)
    else if city == 3 then emit-needle n (512 * q)
    else emit-capitol (emit-monument n (300 * q)) (700 * q)

Section: Entry

  city-seed : Integer -> Integer
  city-seed (city) =
    if city == 0 then 101 else if city == 1 then 203 else if city == 2 then 307
    else if city == 3 then 411 else 523

 build-city fills the vertex buffer for one city and leaves the vertex count
 at 131072 for the page to read. It is the only export the page calls for
 geometry, and it returns the count as well so a caller that already has the
 result does not have to go back to memory for it.

 Back to front, because there is no depth buffer: the page draws this mesh in
 the order it is written and a later triangle wins. The landmark goes last so
 it stands in front of its own city.

 Only the near rank contributes rooftops, so a shell never launches from a
 building on the far bank and appears to come out of the middle of the city.

  build-city : Integer -> Integer
  build-city (city) =
    let s = city-seed city
    in let z = w32 roof-count-addr 0
    in let far = emit-row 0 (0 - 30 * q) (s + 9173) 130 0
    in let z2 = w32 roof-count-addr 0
    in let mid = emit-row far (0 - 30 * q) (s + 4451) 100 1
    in let z3 = w32 roof-count-addr 0
    in let near = emit-row mid (0 - 30 * q) s 80 2
    in let m = emit-landmark near city
    in let w = w32 vtx-count-addr m
    in m

